AWS 5XX Errors — Explained from Real Production Experience 🚨

If your application is running on AWS, 5XX errors mean server-side problems — not user mistakes.

These are some of the most common issues I’ve seen in real production environments:

🔴 500 – Internal Server Error
👉 Application crash, misconfiguration, or unhandled exception

🔴 502 – Bad Gateway
👉 Load balancer reached backend, but backend returned an invalid response
(very common due to wrong ports or app not listening)

🔴 503 – Service Unavailable
👉 No healthy targets available
(auto scaling, deployments, failed health checks)

🔴 504 – Gateway Timeout
👉 Backend took too long to respond
(DB latency, long API execution, timeout mismatch)

📌 Quick thumb rule:
500 = App issue | 502 = Bad backend | 503 = No backend | 504 = Slow backend
Understanding where traffic breaks (DNS → CDN → ALB → App → DB) is the fastest way to debug outages.
